      "headline": "Rules Change Depending on the Field",
      "keyInsight": "Imagine taking a soccer referee to judge a high-end baking contest. The referee blows his whistle because a chef used his hands to knead dough. That sounds ridiculous, right? Yet, traditional thinkers make this blunder every day. They try to judge courtroom trials, dinner recipes, and weather forecasts using the strict rules of pencil-and-paper math. Stephen Toulmin shows that words like \"impossible\" or \"must\" have two separate gears. The core force of the word stays steady, but the measuring tape shifts with the territory. When you say \"you cannot park here,\" you mean a legal city rule. When you say \"iron cannot float in plain water,\" you mean a physical law of nature. Expecting one single ruler to measure every slice of human life is a recipe for total confusion. Smart thinkers never force kitchen standards onto engine repair.",
      "content": "Think about packing a suitcase for a long road trip. You pack heavy coats for snowy mountains and light shirts for sunny beaches. You would never wear ski boots to swim in the ocean. Yet, old-school philosophers tried to force everyone into wearing the exact same intellectual boots everywhere.\n\nTake the humble word \"cannot.\" When an architect says, \"You cannot build a wooden chimney,\" she means the house will burn down. When a judge says, \"You cannot file this lawsuit today,\" she means the legal deadline expired yesterday. The word \"cannot\" carries the same punchy stop sign in both cases. But the reasons behind the stop sign come from two completely different worlds.\n\nEvery walk of life works like a different playing field. What counts as rock-solid proof in a chemistry lab looks very silly in a courtroom. A chemist tests liquids in glass beakers. A jury listens to eyewitness stories and checks alibis. Neither person is wrong. They are simply playing on different fields with different rulebooks.\n\nWhen we argue, we often talk past each other because we mix up the playing fields. If your mechanic says your car battery is dead, do not ask for a geometric proof. Listen to the hum of the alternator instead. Respecting the field helps you solve real problems without needless headaches.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Words like \"must\" or \"cannot\" always set a boundary, but each craft decides where that boundary sits.",
